00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:15.370
How can you tailor your communication,

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:17.730
your presentations
better to your audience.

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:20.720
Just to kick this off, I just
want to briefly talk about

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:23.510
why it is even necessary
to tailor your message

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:24.380
to your audience.

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:27.930
And in the business context,
it is easy to recognize

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:29.280
that you are being confronted

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:31.410
with all kinds of communicative settings.

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:32.790
You might sit together with your team

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:35.260
and talk about the progress
of your individual work,

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:37.170
for instance, directly with your team lead

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:40.190
or you're having a jour-fixe
with members of other teams

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:42.920
of other departments, cross functions,

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:45.890
or even presenting in front
of your senior management.

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:47.960
Maybe in front of your board members.

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:51.730
And I hope it is obvious that
of course the expectations

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:54.240
of your board members,
will be very different

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.700
than the expectations
of your direct teammates

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:58.240
and what you need to say

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:01.770
and how you need to communicate
that with highly differ

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:05.200
based on who you talk to,
based on whom your audience is.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:08.180
So let's start by talking
about a basic framework

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:10.290
which helped me to understand

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:12.440
what the different factors at play are,

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:14.200
what I might need to look out for

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.147
to understand how I
should adapt my message.

00:03:16.147 --> 00:03:18.160
And this basic framework is called

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:20.070
the communication triangle.

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:22.350
And based on this communication triangle

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:25.550
there are things that
you need to be aware of

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:28.060
that you need to take into consideration

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:30.430
when you want to frame
and adopt your message

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:31.263
in the right way.

00:03:31.263 --> 00:03:33.670
And the first dimension of this triangle

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:36.550
is your personal style or
even your company culture.

00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:40.290
Of course, not all presentations
need to look the same.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:41.320
It can differ.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:43.760
Whether it's you presenting
or somebody else.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:47.080
Everybody brings his own
personal style to the table.

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:50.780
And yes, it is completely fine
and completely appropriate

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:53.040
to let your presentation be influenced

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:57.020
by your personal style, by
your way of handling things.

00:03:57.020 --> 00:04:00.210
And on one level further
beyond your personal style,

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:03.460
of course, there's also such
a thing as company culture.

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:04.720
Probably if you work for instance,

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:07.200
one of the GAFA companies
such as Facebook,

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.020
the way the communication is
done will be very different

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:12.940
than for instance, in one of
the old school tech companies

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:14.390
like Siemens in Germany.

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:16.830
So ask yourself, what is
the culture that I am in,

00:04:16.830 --> 00:04:19.290
and also ask yourself,
what is your personal style

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:20.820
that you want to bring to the table?

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:23.200
And then yes, this is the first dimension

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:25.610
of you asking yourself, how you wanna go

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:28.510
about presenting the message
that you want to present.

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:31.217
But of course, in a business
setting, there's more to it

00:04:31.217 --> 00:04:33.230
than just your personal style.

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:37.410
Because there is such a thing
as an objective occasion

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:38.900
that you'll put yourself in.

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:41.570
There are different types
of objective occasions

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:43.460
and as per these different occasions,

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:45.540
there are certain rules
that are just expected

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:48.550
that people will follow
if they put themselves

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:49.480
in these occasions.

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:51.240
And now again, you can
differentiate between

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:54.350
sitting in a boardroom, sitting
together with board members,

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:55.850
and presenting something to them.

00:04:55.850 --> 00:04:58.100
Or just sitting with
your direct colleagues

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:00.330
or maybe with an intern
that you supervise,

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:03.230
and you want to present a
couple of ideas to your intern.

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:06.380
These are all different
objective occasions,

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:08.450
and the way that you present your message,

00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:10.130
the way that you communicate

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:13.660
should be influenced by
this objective occasion.

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:15.630
So again, always ask yourself,

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:17.460
what is your objective occasion

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:19.160
that I'm now putting myself into?

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:21.670
And what likely are the expectations

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:24.470
that are surrounded by
this objective occasion

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:25.660
that you're putting yourself into.

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:29.520
Now the third dimension are
the audience expectations.

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:34.040
Even within one objective
occasion or within one company,

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:35.500
the audience expectations,

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:37.523
the expectations of the different members

00:05:37.523 --> 00:05:40.540
that you are going to talk
to, will be different.

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:43.500
And just one basic example
from my experience.

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:45.610
Now looking at this bottom setting,

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:48.800
are the expectations of
different board room members,

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:51.530
how beautiful, for
instance, PowerPoint pages

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:52.610
need to be made.

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:55.350
So is it fine to just
copy a table for instance,

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:57.690
from an excel sheet into your presentation

00:05:57.690 --> 00:05:59.530
and present that to your board members?

00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:02.120
I've seen many large companies
where many board members

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:03.430
are completely fine with that,

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:05.780
and this is completely
meeting the expectations.

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:08.280
But then I've also seen other companies

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:10.590
where board members just expect

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:13.900
that in the board meetings,
people really do an effort

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:18.510
of making all pages look
beautiful, designing them properly,

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:21.080
and really beautifying all these pages.

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:23.553
So ask yourself now
independent from your culture,

00:06:23.553 --> 00:06:26.330
your personal style, or
the objective occasion,

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:28.710
what will be the
expectations of the people

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:30.740
that you meet, when you sit together,

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:32.520
when you hold your presentation,

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:35.350
and then also take this into consideration

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:36.470
when you're adopting a style.

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:39.510
Now that we have an understanding
of the different factors

00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:41.830
in play about how to tailor your message,

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:44.840
let's think about the typical
documents that you create

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:48.020
and how these considerations
impact these documents.

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:49.650
And let's start by talking about

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:51.750
a simple team alignment document.

00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:53.700
What I envision here is now a setting

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:55.770
where you sit together
with your teammates,

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:58.800
maybe your direct team lead
and you're having a discussion

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:01.410
some kind of an alignment
in your personal work,

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:03.320
maybe you need some
decisions, from your teammates

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:05.580
or some other things
you'd like to talk about,

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:08.280
sitting together and
presenting a basic document.

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:10.030
What's important with these documents

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:12.780
is that you should
rather just be as brief,

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:15.500
as concise as simple as possible.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:18.820
In most settings, don't
waste too much time

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:20.960
for creating beautiful pages.

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:24.660
There is no need to create
elaborated storylines.

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:27.210
So maybe you've seen some of my videos

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:30.390
on good slide writing principles.

00:07:30.390 --> 00:07:31.780
While these are highly relevant

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:33.740
to many of the other
document types we are going

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:34.950
to talk about in a minute.

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:37.260
Don't waste your effort
and energy on that.

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:39.340
This document types will be just be about

00:07:39.340 --> 00:07:41.330
just having an efficient quick meeting

00:07:41.330 --> 00:07:42.650
with your team members.

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:45.470
Just putting a few ideas
on a piece of paper

00:07:45.470 --> 00:07:48.280
and not wasting too
much time thinking about

00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:50.740
the whole presentation and
making beautiful pages.

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:52.830
The second type of documents are so called

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:54.180
jour-fixe documents.

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:57.020
And of course, here it might
change quite significantly

00:07:57.020 --> 00:08:00.280
company by company, what
exactly jour-fixe is,

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:03.290
how this is handled, what
type of people sit in.

00:08:03.290 --> 00:08:05.820
And so please adjust that
based on these factors.

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:08.480
But in general jour-fixes
are used to sit together

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:11.230
with people also from other
teams, other departments

00:08:11.230 --> 00:08:13.890
other functions and align on the progress

00:08:13.890 --> 00:08:16.830
on certain projects or
other important initiatives

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:17.860
that you are working on.

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:20.880
And here, I still believe
that the focus should not be

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:23.510
too much about creating beautiful pages.

00:08:23.510 --> 00:08:26.570
These should be rather
very hands on meetings

00:08:26.570 --> 00:08:28.610
where you sit together
with the people aligning

00:08:28.610 --> 00:08:30.030
and just getting the perspectives.

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:32.330
Though in contrast to these documents

00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:36.350
that just stay in your team in
the safe space of your team.

00:08:36.350 --> 00:08:38.250
With jour-fixe documents like that

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:41.280
here, quality is now much more important

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:43.200
for the reason that these documents

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:46.110
will be easily sent
around the departments.

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:48.710
Other people might see
and read these documents

00:08:48.710 --> 00:08:50.630
who weren't even sitting in the meeting.

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:54.100
And here it's not very easy
quick to just confuse people.

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:56.790
If your pages are not set up properly,

00:08:56.790 --> 00:09:01.240
if your pages do not follow
good slide writting practices,

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:03.300
then you will have very quickly problem

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:04.930
and people will be confused.

00:09:04.930 --> 00:09:07.550
And this might even create
some political issues

00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:09.420
in your company, in your organization,

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:11.280
if you don't work it in the right way.

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:12.740
So if you want to know more about

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:15.940
what these good slide
writing principles are

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:17.910
that I would highly recommend you,

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:20.690
I have a whole playlist on
this channel with videos

00:09:20.690 --> 00:09:22.270
that I created on this topic,

00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:24.230
I will link it somewhere above here.

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:27.720
So check that out for these
good slide writing practices.

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:29.600
And if you're really serious

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:32.130
to improve your slide creation game

00:09:32.130 --> 00:09:35.190
and create business presentations
that follow the standards

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:38.690
of top consulting firms
such as BCG or McKinsey,

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:41.870
I created a whole online
course on the topic.

00:09:41.870 --> 00:09:45.530
I have over 10,000 students
already taking that course.

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:47.020
So if you believe this is something

00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:48.470
that might interest you as well,

00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:50.520
I have a discount link to the course

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:52.140
in the video description below.

00:09:52.140 --> 00:09:53.530
So feel free to check it out.

00:09:53.530 --> 00:09:56.140
There's also lots of student
reviews for you to read

00:09:56.140 --> 00:09:57.040
to better understand.

00:09:57.040 --> 00:09:58.890
One final tip that I have for you

00:09:58.890 --> 00:10:01.180
regarding these Jour Fixe documents,

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:02.700
make sure that in the beginning,

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:04.400
when you set up a Jour Fixe,

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:06.750
you will line on a basic agenda

00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:08.940
with the other members of the Jour Fixe

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:11.110
so that you already have a skeleton

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:12.857
for the document aligned.

00:10:12.857 --> 00:10:14.440
And you don't need to think about

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:17.710
every time we have these
weekly jour fixes from scratch,

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:19.873
how you structure it, what the things are,

00:10:19.873 --> 00:10:21.420
that you should put into it.

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:24.550
Next, this board-level type of documents

00:10:24.550 --> 00:10:27.540
or other types of documents
that you are going to present

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:30.960
in front of senior leadership
of your organization.

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:34.990
Here now all these slide writing
and story lining principles

00:10:34.990 --> 00:10:37.450
that are talked so much about
and all these other videos

00:10:37.450 --> 00:10:39.010
and also my online course,

00:10:39.010 --> 00:10:41.540
this is where you really need to nail it.

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:43.570
This is where this is so important.

00:10:43.570 --> 00:10:46.390
Just put yourself into the
shoes of the board member

00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:48.230
of a large blue chip company,

00:10:48.230 --> 00:10:52.840
you will likely have 20,
30, 40, 50 projects going on

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:53.950
in parallel.

00:10:53.950 --> 00:10:57.410
All these initiatives, all
these teams he's responsible for

00:10:57.410 --> 00:10:59.800
and that are regularly
presenting updates to him.

00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:02.880
It is so easy to get completely derailed

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:04.390
in these kinds of meetings

00:11:04.390 --> 00:11:06.470
for little minor details

00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:08.660
and how your presentation is structured

00:11:08.660 --> 00:11:11.680
for little in accuracies on your pages.

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:13.510
He'll be super diligent

00:11:13.510 --> 00:11:16.000
when you create your
document to really meet

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:19.270
first the objective occasion
of these kind of meetings,

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:21.250
but then also the expectations

00:11:21.250 --> 00:11:23.870
that many of these senior managers...

00:11:23.870 --> 00:11:26.220
many of these senior leaders will have.

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:27.620
When I worked at McKinsey,

00:11:27.620 --> 00:11:29.187
one of the partners used to tell me,

00:11:29.187 --> 00:11:31.420
"Heinrich when you
present at board meetings,

00:11:31.420 --> 00:11:34.560
you to see to keep it
as simple as possible.

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:36.890
Use very basic language.

00:11:36.890 --> 00:11:40.380
Do not use any fancy
complicated technical terms,

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:42.970
use high font sizes on your page.

00:11:42.970 --> 00:11:45.390
Don't use very little nitty-gritty text

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:47.570
that nobody's going to read anyway,

00:11:47.570 --> 00:11:49.840
keep it simple and to the point

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:51.690
and this is then where you will win

00:11:51.690 --> 00:11:52.900
in these kinds of meetings."

00:11:52.900 --> 00:11:53.957
Next are documents that

00:11:53.957 --> 00:11:56.450
are created for an external audience.

00:11:56.450 --> 00:11:58.640
For instance, and Investor Relations

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:01.930
or if you sit together with
your banks, lending institutions

00:12:01.930 --> 00:12:05.280
or any other occasion
where external stakeholders

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:06.113
are involved.

00:12:06.113 --> 00:12:07.740
And here, I believe that the principles

00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:09.440
that you need to apply are very close

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:11.950
to what is expected in
the board level setting.

00:12:11.950 --> 00:12:14.210
Meaning that of course,
you should hold yourself

00:12:14.210 --> 00:12:16.660
to the highest quality
standards out there.

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:20.020
But on top of that, be aware
that these external documents

00:12:20.020 --> 00:12:22.560
often have legal implications.

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:25.270
For instance, if you work
in the finance department,

00:12:25.270 --> 00:12:28.160
you need to be very careful
what claims you make

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:30.090
around the liquidity of your company,

00:12:30.090 --> 00:12:31.880
especially maybe if your company

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:34.210
is in a tough financial
position at the moment.

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:36.970
So make sure that you
consult with your legal team,

00:12:36.970 --> 00:12:39.280
with your PR team with
all the other experts

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:40.630
that you have in your organization

00:12:40.630 --> 00:12:42.240
to make sure that your documents

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:43.800
meet these higher standards.

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:46.340
And last but not least,
let's talk about documents

00:12:46.340 --> 00:12:48.700
that are mainly used for documentation.

00:12:48.700 --> 00:12:51.710
So these are documents which
may never get presented,

00:12:51.710 --> 00:12:54.480
but it's rather about
documenting certain things,

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:57.400
how things work, how certain
processes are set up.

00:12:57.400 --> 00:12:58.940
And what's of course important here

00:12:58.940 --> 00:13:00.890
is not to really get into the details

00:13:00.890 --> 00:13:02.650
and be super specific and detailed.

00:13:02.650 --> 00:13:05.840
In contrasting that with board
level style of presentations

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:08.010
where you use very high font sizes

00:13:08.010 --> 00:13:10.220
where you try to not get
too much into the details,

00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:12.190
where you try to not get too complicated

00:13:12.190 --> 00:13:13.730
with these documentation documents,

00:13:13.730 --> 00:13:15.470
of course, it's going to be lots of text,

00:13:15.470 --> 00:13:17.610
of course, you're going
to use lots of words,

00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:20.510
you're going to make a very
detailed and very specific

00:13:20.510 --> 00:13:23.180
because this is what this
document is all about.

00:13:23.180 --> 00:13:25.720
And here even more important
than with other documents

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:28.310
is that this document
needs to stand for itself.

00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:30.160
And what this means is
that maybe if somebody

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:32.100
one year from now or two years from now

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:34.530
picks up this document,
and maybe all the members

00:13:34.530 --> 00:13:36.530
who worked on the document
in the first place

00:13:36.530 --> 00:13:38.540
are not in the organization anymore.

00:13:38.540 --> 00:13:40.550
And this person is able to understand

00:13:40.550 --> 00:13:43.530
everything thing out of
the document in itself

00:13:43.530 --> 00:13:45.900
without anybody needing
to stand next to it

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:46.810
and to explain it to him.

00:13:46.810 --> 00:13:49.050
So the document needs to stand for itself,

00:13:49.050 --> 00:13:50.560
needs to be self-sufficient

00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:52.600
in the way that information is presented.

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:53.980
So these were my thoughts

00:13:53.980 --> 00:13:55.793
on how you adapt your presentations,

00:13:55.793 --> 00:13:58.020
your documents to your audience,

00:13:58.020 --> 00:13:59.910
very much hope this makes sense.

